@node	0.8, 0.9, Chapter 0, Chapter 0
@section Errors

@noindent
This book distinguishes two kinds of failure, and gives each its own
mechanism. A condition that is impossible if every earlier part of the
program is correct (a broken internal invariant, a case a caller was told never to reach) is an @newterm{exception}: it stops the program (or a
handler further up the call chain) rather than trying to compute a sensible
result from nonsense. A condition that is a normal, expected possibility of
the operation itself (a string that might not parse as a number, a division that might be by zero) is a value the caller must handle, using
@code{option} or @code{result}. Which of the two applies is a design
decision, not a matter of taste, and this edition applies it consistently
throughout.

@subsubheading Exceptions, for violated invariants

@code{exception} declares a new exception constructor, @code{raise} throws
it, and an unhandled exception stops the program with a message naming it:

@example
# exception Empty_queue;;
exception Empty_queue
# let dequeue = function
    | [] -> raise Empty_queue
    | head :: tail -> head, tail;;
val dequeue : 'a list -> 'a * 'a list = <fun>
# dequeue [ 1; 2 ];;
- : int * int list = (1, [2])
# dequeue ([] : int list);;
Exception: Empty_queue.
@end example

@noindent
@code{dequeue}'s caller is expected to know the queue is nonempty before
calling; dequeuing an empty queue is a bug at the call site, not a
circumstance to route around, so it raises rather than returning some
placeholder. @code{assert} is the same idea applied to a condition stated
inline rather than as a separate @code{match} arm; it raises @code{Assert_failure}, naming the exact source location, if the asserted
condition is false:

@example
# let checked_sqrt n =
    assert (n >= 0);
    sqrt (float_of_int n)
  ;;
val checked_sqrt : int -> float = <fun>
# checked_sqrt 9;;
- : float = 3.
# checked_sqrt (-1);;
Exception: Assert_failure ("//toplevel//", 2, 2).
@end example

@noindent
@code{(2, 2)} names the line and column of the @code{assert} inside the phrase just typed; a real program's assertion failure names a file and
line the same way, pointing straight at the violated invariant. This
edition never writes a bare @code{try @dots{} with _ ->}: catching every
exception indiscriminately, including ones the catching code has no way to
have anticipated, throws away exactly the information @code{Assert_failure}
and its cousins exist to preserve.

@subsubheading @code{result}, for expected failure

A function whose failure is a normal outcome of its input, not a broken
invariant, returns @code{('a, 'error) result}: @code{Ok @var{value}} on
success, @code{Error @var{error}} on the expected kind of failure. A closed
variant of possible errors, rather than a bare string, lets a caller match
on exactly which failure occurred:

@example
# type step_error =
    | Bad_number of string
    | Odd of int;;
type step_error = Bad_number of string | Odd of int
# let parse_int s =
    match int_of_string_opt s with
    | Some n -> Ok n
    | None -> Error (Bad_number s);;
val parse_int : string -> (int, step_error) result = <fun>
# let halve_even n = if n mod 2 = 0 then Ok (n / 2) else Error (Odd n);;
val halve_even : int -> (int, step_error) result = <fun>
@end example

@subsubheading Chaining fallible steps with @code{Result.bind}

Three steps that each might fail compose with @code{Result.bind}, which
runs the next step only if the previous one succeeded, and short-circuits
to the first @code{Error} otherwise: this is this edition's whole answer
to the question a long chain of error checks would otherwise
answer by hand:

@example
# let label n = Ok (string_of_int n ^ " is half of the input");;
val label : int -> (string, 'a) result = <fun>
# let message = function
    | Bad_number s -> "not a number: " ^ s
    | Odd n -> string_of_int n ^ " is odd";;
val message : step_error -> string = <fun>
# let half_report s =
    Result.bind (Result.bind (parse_int s) halve_even) label
    |> Result.map_error message;;
val half_report : string -> (string, string) result = <fun>
# half_report "20";;
- : (string, string) result = Ok "10 is half of the input"
# half_report "7";;
- : (string, string) result = Error "7 is odd"
# half_report "x";;
- : (string, string) result = Error "not a number: x"
@end example

@noindent
@code{Result.bind (Result.bind (parse_int s) halve_even) label} reads
inside-out: parse, then (if parsing succeeded) halve, then (if halving
succeeded) label. @code{Result.map_error} runs only on the @code{Error} side,
turning whichever @code{step_error} stopped the chain into one printable
line; a caller of @code{half_report} sees exactly one of a success string or
a failure string, never a raised exception, because nothing about a bad
input to this function is an invariant violation: it is the input a string of unknown provenance is expected to sometimes be.

Exercise 0.5 is the same chain with every error already collapsed to plain
@code{string}, and chapter 4's evaluator leans on this same
@code{result}-chaining shape wherever a computation can fail for a reason
the caller should see, not merely a reason the program should stop for.

@subsubheading Exercise

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 0.5}Exercise 0.5:} Chain three fallible steps with
@code{Result.bind}: parse a year of age from a string, check that it lies
between @code{0} and @code{150}, and describe it in a sentence. Then map
whichever @code{Error} stops the chain into one printable summary string, so
that a caller of @code{age_summary} sees a readable sentence either way.
@end quotation

